          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is I10 a complete code, or does it need a fourth character?",
          "answer": "I10 is a complete code. It has three characters and does not take a fourth. The source is the coding document \"Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family,\" which says that most ICD-10-CM categories need a fourth, fifth or sixth character, but I10 takes none. It also says that asking to \"add more specificity\" to I10 cannot be satisfied by the classification.\n\nI10 is the code for essential (primary) hypertension, which is high blood pressure described as arterial, essential, primary or systemic. The same document notes that the older split between benign and malignant hypertension is not carried into this code.\n\nThe general code-structure document (\"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection\") explains that longer codes add detail such as cause, site or severity, which is why I10 needs no extension.\n\nThe passages I found do not cover what a clinician would consider when choosing a code for a particular patient, or how to code hypertension that is linked to heart or kidney disease. Those are in the combination codes I11 to I16, which the sources only mention briefly. A medical coder or clinician can confirm which code fits a specific record.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
